## COMMON INTERVIEW QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS

---

Q: Why did you choose Spring Boot over other frameworks?
A: "Spring Boot reduces boilerplate configuration significantly.
It has auto-configuration, embedded Tomcat server, and starter dependencies.
For a REST API project like MediDoc, I can focus on business logic
rather than infrastructure setup."

---

Q: What is JPA and Hibernate?
A: "JPA is Java Persistence API — a specification for mapping Java objects
to database tables. Hibernate is the implementation Spring Boot uses by default.
Instead of writing SQL, I define Java entities with annotations like
@Entity, @Table, @Column and Hibernate generates the SQL automatically.
For example, Patient.java maps directly to the patients table."

---

Q: What is the difference between @RestController and @Controller?
A: "@Controller returns a view — like an HTML page.
@RestController returns data — JSON or XML.
In MediDoc all my controllers are @RestController because
I'm building REST APIs that the frontend fetches with JavaScript."

---

Q: What is a DTO and why do you use it?
A: "DTO stands for Data Transfer Object. It defines the exact shape
of data coming in from or going out to the client.
I keep DTOs separate from entities for two reasons:
First, security — I don't want to expose internal fields like passwords.
Second, flexibility — the API shape can change without affecting the database model."

---

Q: How does Spring Security work in your project?
A: "Spring Security intercepts every HTTP request.
I configured it to allow public routes like /api/auth/** without authentication.
Protected routes require a valid JWT token in the Authorization header.
My JwtAuthFilter reads the token, validates it, extracts the user role,
and sets the authentication in SecurityContextHolder.
Spring Security then checks if the role matches the route permission."

---

Q: What is the difference between authentication and authorization?
A: "Authentication is verifying WHO you are — login with OTP or password.
Authorization is verifying WHAT you can do — a patient cannot access
doctor-only routes even after login.
In MediDoc, authentication happens at /api/auth/patient-login.
Authorization is handled by Spring Security checking the JWT role claim
against the route permissions in SecurityConfig."

---

Q: How did you handle password security?
A: "For the demo, passwords are stored as plain text for simplicity.
In production, I would use BCryptPasswordEncoder which is already
configured as a @Bean in SecurityConfig.java.
BCrypt applies a one-way hash with salt so even if the database
is compromised, passwords cannot be reversed."

---

Q: What is CORS and why did you configure it?
A: "CORS is Cross-Origin Resource Sharing.
When my frontend at localhost:5500 calls my backend at localhost:8080,
the browser blocks it because they are on different ports — different origins.
I added @CrossOrigin(origins = '*') on controllers and configured
CORS in SecurityConfig to allow the frontend to call the backend.
In production, I would restrict this to specific domains."

---

Q: What was the hardest part of this project?
A: "The hardest part was configuring JWT with Spring Security.
Spring Security by default blocks all requests.
I had to correctly order the JwtAuthFilter before the default
UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter, disable CSRF for REST APIs,
set session management to STATELESS, and correctly extract
the role from the token to set authentication in SecurityContext.
It took multiple attempts to get the filter chain order right."

---

Q: What would you improve if given more time?
A: "Three things:
First, replace simulated OTP with a real SMS gateway like MSG91 or Twilio.
Second, replace plain text passwords with BCrypt hashing.
Third, add refresh token support so users don't get logged out after 24 hours.
I would also add comprehensive unit tests using JUnit and Mockito."

